==============================================================================
(1) THE DEMOCRATIC GRAPH IN CIRCULANT FORM
==============================================================================
  The graph's Hessian is (N+1) I - J. On the 3-cycle J = I + P + P^2, so
      H = 4I - (I + P + P^2) = 3I - P - P^2,   i.e.  a = 3, b = -1  (REAL).

    mode q      eps_q
  --------------------
         0   1.000000
         1   4.000000
         2   4.000000

  eps_1 - eps_2 = -1.332e-15   -- the doublet is EXACTLY degenerate

  NOTE THE CONVENTION TRAP. This (a, b) is the CIRCULANT pair and is not T6's (a, b),
  which is (on-site, bond) in H = (1/2) sum [a f_k^2 + b (f_k - f_{k+1})^2]. The graph
  gives on-site = bond = g, i.e. T6's a = b; in circulant form the same operator reads
  a = 3g, b = -g. Two different pairs of letters for two different decompositions of one
  matrix. Quoting 'a = b' across the two rooms without saying which is a live error.

==============================================================================
(2) THE PHASE IS A FLAT DIRECTION — CHECKED, NOT ASSERTED
==============================================================================
  Build the ring profile f_k = M + R cos(phi + 2 pi k/3) at several phi, all with the
  same M and R, and evaluate the graph energy E = (1/2) f^T H f. If phi is flat, E does
  not move; and Q, which is what the mechanism predicts, must not move either.

         phi         masses (f_k^2), normalised            E            Q
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
     0.00000             0.9714  0.0143  0.0143     7.500000  0.666666667
     0.22222             0.9436  0.0003  0.0561     7.500000  0.666666667
     0.40000             0.8836  0.0027  0.1136     7.500000  0.666666667
     0.26180             0.9330  0.0000  0.0670     7.500000  0.666666667
     1.00000             0.5187  0.0284  0.4529     7.500000  0.666666667
     2.00000             0.0282  0.0054  0.9663     7.500000  0.666666667

  E is constant to 4.44e-16 and Q to 4.44e-16 across the whole range,
  while the individual mass fractions swing over the full spectrum. So the mechanism
  determines Q and determines NOTHING about which masses realise it.

==============================================================================
(3) A COMPLEX BOND DOES NOT FIX IT EITHER
==============================================================================
  The tempting next move is a complex hopping: in circulant form the charged stiffnesses
  are a + 2|b| cos(arg b +- 2 pi/3), which do split once arg b is neither 0 nor pi.

       arg b       eps_1       eps_2        split
  ------------------------------------------------
     0.00000    4.000000    4.000000    1.332e-15
     0.22222    4.357150    3.593670    7.635e-01
     0.52360    4.732051    3.000000    1.732e+00
     3.14159    2.000000    2.000000    1.776e-15

  But the splitting does NOT reach phi, because the ring field is REAL: f_2 = f_1*, so
  |f_1| = |f_2| and only the SUM of the two stiffnesses enters the energy,

      E = eps_0 f_0^2 + (eps_1 + eps_2) |f_1|^2,   eps_1 + eps_2 = 2a - 2 Re b,

  which carries no phi at all. Checked directly:

       arg b      E(phi=0)    E(phi=2/9)      E(phi=1)      spread
  ----------------------------------------------------------------
     0.00000     15.000000     15.000000     15.000000    8.88e-15
     0.22222     15.073770     15.073770     15.073770    8.88e-15
     0.52360     15.401924     15.401924     15.401924    8.88e-15
     1.30000     17.197504     17.197504     17.197504    3.55e-15

  So EVERY Z3-symmetric quadratic form leaves phi flat, complex bond or not. The phase
  cannot be reached at quadratic order by anything.

==============================================================================
(4) THE CUBIC SEES phi — BUT ONLY THROUGH cos(3 phi)
==============================================================================
  The lowest term that sees phi is the Z3-invariant cubic, and its phi-dependence is
  exactly one harmonic:

      sum_k f_k^3 = 3 M^3 + (9/2) M R^2 + (3/4) R^3 cos(3 phi)

        phi           direct      closed form       diff
  ------------------------------------------------------
     0.0000     14.121320344     14.121320344    5.3e-15
     0.2222     13.667118634     13.667118634    1.8e-15
     1.0000      9.899908777      9.899908777    3.6e-15
     2.0000     14.036828762     14.036828762    1.8e-15

  That is the 3-phi object the closure is about -- and it is also the trap. A single
  cos(3 phi) is stationary only at 3 phi = 0 and 3 phi = pi, so a pure cubic selects
  phi in {0, +-2pi/3} or {pi/3, pi, -pi/3}. The closure needs 3 phi = Q = 0.666667,
  which misses the nearer of the two by 0.6667 rad. A cubic CANNOT deliver it.

  Nor can any higher order, without tuning. Z3 symmetry makes the potential invariant
  under phi -> phi + 2pi/3, and reflection makes it even in phi, so ANY real symmetric
  ring potential is a function V = F(cos 3 phi) alone. Its stationary points are
  sin 3 phi = 0 -- the same two -- plus the roots of F'(cos 3 phi) = 0, which sit at
  whatever the couplings are tuned to put them. Landing one on cos Q = 0.785887
  is fitting the answer, not deriving it.

==============================================================================
VERDICT — THE TWO DEBTS ARE DIFFERENT IN KIND
==============================================================================
  The koide_relation file states the debt as 'one number and one relation'. They are now
  cleanly separated, and their difficulty is not shared:

    THE NUMBER (why Q = 2/3): a statement about a RATIO OF MODULI. The democratic graph
      supplies a candidate, and Q is phase-blind, so that candidate is complete on its
      own terms -- it does not owe the phase as a missing piece.

    THE RELATION (why 3 arg f_1 = Q): unreachable from the ring's own real potential at
      ANY order. Quadratic terms leave phi exactly flat; cubic and higher see it only
      through cos(3 phi), whose natural stationary points are 0 and pi. Producing
      3 phi = Q requires an EXTERNAL PHASE REFERENCE that carries the angle Q itself.

  That is why the corpus's route for it is thermal rather than potential-based: the KMS
  construction supplies exactly such a reference, the Euclidean time circle, and reads
  the angle as a twist per face. Whether that reference is legitimate is task #2's real
  question. What is settled here is that nothing internal to the ring can answer it --
  which retires every candidate built from the ring's own couplings, at every order.
